FTP(1)                         P32W Unix                          FTP(1)

NAME
       ftp - move files to and from another computer

SYNOPSIS
       ftp [-n] [host [port]]

DESCRIPTION
       ftp connects to an FTP server (port 21 unless given) and takes
       commands, one a line, at the prompt ftp>. On connecting it asks
       for a name and a password, unless -n is given; then user logs
       in. The server's numbered replies are shown as they come.

       open host [port]  connect
       user name [pass]  log in (anonymous takes any password)
       ls [path]         the server's directory, long; nlist: names only
       cd path, cdup, pwd
       get remote [local]
                         a file from the server (named as on the
                         server, less its directories, unless local)
       put local [remote]
                         a file to the server
       delete path, mkdir path, rmdir path
       binary            files move as they are (the default)
       ascii             text: CR LF on the network, LF on P32W
       status, close, quit (or bye), help (or ?)

       After a transfer ftp says how many bytes moved, and how fast.

       Every transfer uses passive mode: the server names a port and ftp
       connects to it, at the address of the control connection. A
       server behind a router often names an address only its own
       network can reach; ftp does not use it.

EXAMPLES
       ftp -n 10.0.2.2 2121
       ftp> user anonymous me@p32w
       ftp> get README

SEE ALSO
       fetch(1), telnet(1), socket(2)

BUGS
       No active mode, no mget or mput, no resumed transfers. FTP is not
       encrypted: names and passwords cross the network as they are.
